In preliminary 2026 Senate data, Republican two-party vote shares in 30 of 32 eligible states—excluding Alaska and Montana—closely follow approximately 0.85 × Trump’s 2024 two-party share. The prediction error is about 2.95 percentage points. Why such consistency across different states and candidates?

The five historical midterms with the closest-sized Republican declines had prediction errors of 7.85–12.75 points. Including all 32 eligible 2026 races raises the error to 5.75 points. These comparisons deserve a transparent explanation and verification of the underlying counts.

The pattern also appears in statewide House totals for those same 30 states: a multiplier of approximately 0.855, with a prediction error of 2.57 points. That gives us a concrete pattern to examine across two kinds of contests.

The attached chart adds the ballot-measure comparison. The first three panels show differences between support for conservative ballot positions and Republican House candidates in 2018, 2022 and 2026. Yellow identifies states belonging to the selected 2026 Senate group. The fourth panel illustrates a hypothetical subtraction of 15 percentage points from those yellow bars’ gaps. It is an illustration—not an observed counting discrepancy or a reversal of the 0.85 formula.

@DataRepublican’s pre-election recordings also deserve public answers. Participants discussed an election-legitimacy crisis, an Insurrection Act response, governors taking a larger governing role and, in some statements, extra-constitutional change. Who endorsed those proposals, and what authority did they believe they had? Those accountability questions are separate from whether votes were counted accurately, but both deserve clear answers.

For voters whose trust has weakened since 2020, another online argument will not settle this. A transparent paper-ballot check offers something tangible. Use bipartisan observers, documented ballot custody and published comparisons. If the paper ballots match the reported totals, that strengthens confidence. If material discrepancies appear, investigate them.

@IowaSOS @NCSBE @TXsecofstate @KansasSOS—please publish the results of existing paper-ballot audits and explain and use the lawful hand-audit or recount mechanisms available for these races, including appropriate additional verification in Iowa, North Carolina, Texas and Kansas.

The data does not establish fraud. The request is verification: demonstrate that the reported totals match the paper ballots, regardless of which party benefits.

